WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.140
Lesson one point four.

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.590
How much time the laptop has, and how we

3
00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:03.840
know.

4
00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:10.150
In lesson one point three the loop slept

5
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:11.930
until D t, the decision time.

6
00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:13.825
Here's where it comes from.

7
00:00:14.169 --> 00:00:16.638
Start from S t, the moment slot t has to

8
00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:16.982
sound.

9
00:00:17.461 --> 00:00:19.786
Slot t is decided from the guitar up to

10
00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:20.774
slot t minus one.

11
00:00:21.123 --> 00:00:23.739
Plucks are rounded to the nearest slot, so

12
00:00:23.739 --> 00:00:26.123
slot t minus one collects every pluck up

13
00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:27.634
to half a slot before S t.

14
00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:30.308
At a hundred and twenty, that edge is

15
00:00:30.308 --> 00:00:32.692
sixty-two and a half milliseconds before.

16
00:00:33.162 --> 00:00:35.203
A pluck right at that edge reaches the

17
00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:37.453
laptop a little later, so it waits a set

18
00:00:37.453 --> 00:00:39.127
delay: twenty milliseconds.

19
00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:41.743
Then a five-millisecond margin, in case a

20
00:00:41.743 --> 00:00:42.528
packet is slow.

21
00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:44.374
That's the decision time.

22
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.904
What's left between deciding and sounding

23
00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:49.486
is the window, W: sixty-two and a half,

24
00:00:49.486 --> 00:00:52.122
minus twenty, minus five: thirty-seven and

25
00:00:52.122 --> 00:00:54.072
a half milliseconds at a hundred and

26
00:00:54.072 --> 00:00:54.440
twenty.

27
00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.693
Inside the window the laptop has to step

28
00:00:59.693 --> 00:01:01.107
the model and get the sound out.

29
00:01:01.373 --> 00:01:03.229
The audio system reports twenty-two point

30
00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.086
six five milliseconds from handing over a

31
00:01:05.086 --> 00:01:06.501
sound to it leaving the speaker.

32
00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.579
Sound goes out in buffers of two hundred

33
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:10.435
and fifty-six samples, five point eight

34
00:01:10.435 --> 00:01:12.425
milliseconds each, and the code allows one

35
00:01:12.425 --> 00:01:12.955
more buffer.

36
00:01:13.221 --> 00:01:15.033
Plus two milliseconds for the model step.

37
00:01:15.298 --> 00:01:16.625
Thirty point four five in all.

38
00:01:17.047 --> 00:01:18.943
Now set that against the window at three

39
00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:19.267
tempos.

40
00:01:19.544 --> 00:01:21.624
At a hundred beats per minute, the planned

41
00:01:21.624 --> 00:01:23.705
exhibition tempo, there are nineteen and a

42
00:01:23.705 --> 00:01:24.953
half milliseconds to spare.

43
00:01:25.231 --> 00:01:26.756
At a hundred and twenty, seven.

44
00:01:27.034 --> 00:01:29.299
At about a hundred and thirty-five, none:

45
00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:30.640
the window and the need meet.

46
00:01:30.917 --> 00:01:32.674
The start-up line warns above about a

47
00:01:32.674 --> 00:01:33.830
hundred and twenty-eight.

48
00:01:37.213 --> 00:01:39.042
A timing measurement is thousands of

49
00:01:39.042 --> 00:01:39.438
numbers.

50
00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:41.860
Here are nine made-up ones, to learn the

51
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:42.157
words.

52
00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:43.080
Sort them.

53
00:01:43.388 --> 00:01:45.648
The middle one is the median: half the

54
00:01:45.648 --> 00:01:46.520
steps are faster.

55
00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:48.728
A percentile says the same for other

56
00:01:48.728 --> 00:01:51.090
shares: the ninety-ninth percentile, p

57
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.350
ninety-nine, is the time ninety-nine in a

58
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.325
hundred steps beat.

59
00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:56.739
And the worst case is the single slowest.

60
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.086
Here are the real numbers for the

61
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.250
transformer drummer, from every live

62
00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.082
session so far.

63
00:02:03.415 --> 00:02:05.635
On this axis each step is ten times the

64
00:02:05.635 --> 00:02:07.909
last, so small and huge times both fit.

65
00:02:08.242 --> 00:02:10.517
Median one point seven four milliseconds.

66
00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.903
p ninety-five, two point five five.

67
00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:15.510
p ninety-nine, about three point seven.

68
00:02:15.843 --> 00:02:18.118
The dashed lines are how long a step can

69
00:02:18.118 --> 00:02:20.781
take before its sound would be late: about

70
00:02:20.781 --> 00:02:23.278
nine milliseconds at a hundred and twenty,

71
00:02:23.278 --> 00:02:24.610
twenty-one at a hundred.

72
00:02:24.942 --> 00:02:27.606
And the worst: seventy-one milliseconds,

73
00:02:27.606 --> 00:02:29.270
longer than that take's whole

74
00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.657
fifty-millisecond window.

75
00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.546
That slot happened to have no drums in it,

76
00:02:33.546 --> 00:02:34.596
so nothing was lost.

77
00:02:34.911 --> 00:02:36.853
But this is why the median isn't the

78
00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:39.267
story: the slot that matters is the slow

79
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:40.369
one with a hit in it.

80
00:02:43.927 --> 00:02:45.934
Here's the same step timed two ways.

81
00:02:46.269 --> 00:02:48.779
In green, offline, slots stepped back to

82
00:02:48.779 --> 00:02:49.058
back.

83
00:02:49.392 --> 00:02:52.013
In ochre, in the live loop, one step every

84
00:02:52.013 --> 00:02:54.356
hundred and twenty to a hundred and fifty

85
00:02:54.356 --> 00:02:55.081
milliseconds.

86
00:02:55.415 --> 00:02:57.702
Every model is several times slower live.

87
00:02:58.189 --> 00:02:58.398
Why?

88
00:02:58.713 --> 00:03:00.966
Between slots the processor core sits idle

89
00:03:00.966 --> 00:03:03.481
and goes cold: most likely its clock slows

90
00:03:03.481 --> 00:03:05.682
and its caches empty, though we haven't

91
00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:06.468
measured which.

92
00:03:06.782 --> 00:03:08.878
The same step costs zero point one four

93
00:03:08.878 --> 00:03:11.131
milliseconds back to back, and one point

94
00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:12.022
six after a rest.

95
00:03:12.337 --> 00:03:14.642
The lesson: time the loop as it really

96
00:03:14.642 --> 00:03:14.904
runs.

97
00:03:15.761 --> 00:03:18.525
And a trap: the laptop's GPU is seventeen

98
00:03:18.525 --> 00:03:20.819
times slower for one slot than one CPU

99
00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:21.231
thread.

100
00:03:21.584 --> 00:03:24.407
That's latency against throughput: the GPU

101
00:03:24.407 --> 00:03:26.936
is built for thousands of slots at once,

102
00:03:26.936 --> 00:03:27.995
which is training.

103
00:03:28.348 --> 00:03:30.818
Live needs eight answers a second, each

104
00:03:30.818 --> 00:03:31.348
one fast.

105
00:03:34.863 --> 00:03:36.442
The loop has to wake up at D t.

106
00:03:36.748 --> 00:03:38.581
Asked to sleep a hundred and twenty

107
00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:40.873
milliseconds, the Mac woke about six and a

108
00:03:40.873 --> 00:03:42.707
half milliseconds late, typically.

109
00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:44.948
That comes straight out of the window.

110
00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:47.266
The fix: never sleep long.

111
00:03:47.556 --> 00:03:49.587
Sleep a quarter of what's left, at most

112
00:03:49.587 --> 00:03:51.859
twenty milliseconds, again and again, then

113
00:03:51.859 --> 00:03:53.841
spend the last few milliseconds checking

114
00:03:53.841 --> 00:03:55.098
the clock in a tight loop.

115
00:03:55.388 --> 00:03:57.031
Now it wakes two thousandths of a

116
00:03:57.031 --> 00:03:57.853
millisecond late.

117
00:03:58.143 --> 00:04:00.319
Rare long stalls still happen, and they're

118
00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:00.657
logged.

119
00:04:04.893 --> 00:04:06.848
How long does sound really take to get

120
00:04:06.848 --> 00:04:07.801
out, and back in?

121
00:04:08.101 --> 00:04:10.006
We played a tick from the speaker and

122
00:04:10.006 --> 00:04:12.262
recorded it on the microphone, on the same

123
00:04:12.262 --> 00:04:12.563
clock.

124
00:04:13.001 --> 00:04:14.962
The audio system's own timestamps said

125
00:04:14.962 --> 00:04:16.118
eight milliseconds out.

126
00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:18.380
Its reported latencies said twenty-two

127
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.335
point six five out.

128
00:04:19.637 --> 00:04:21.798
The tick came back forty-three point three

129
00:04:21.798 --> 00:04:23.759
milliseconds later than the timestamps

130
00:04:23.759 --> 00:04:25.669
predicted, and the reported figures

131
00:04:25.669 --> 00:04:27.278
predicted forty-four point four.

132
00:04:27.579 --> 00:04:29.540
So the reported numbers are right as a

133
00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.400
sum, and the timestamps were wrong.

134
00:04:31.829 --> 00:04:34.205
Only the sum was measured, so twenty-three

135
00:04:34.205 --> 00:04:36.264
milliseconds out is still the system's

136
00:04:36.264 --> 00:04:37.214
split, not ours.

137
00:04:37.531 --> 00:04:39.590
And on Bluetooth earbuds it reported a

138
00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.125
hundred and eighty: which is why the drums

139
00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:43.392
never go over Bluetooth.

140
00:04:45.279 --> 00:04:47.082
Jitter is how much a time wanders.

141
00:04:47.401 --> 00:04:49.575
The real board's onset packets, on the

142
00:04:49.575 --> 00:04:51.855
fourteenth of October: ninety percent

143
00:04:51.855 --> 00:04:54.030
arrived between half a millisecond early

144
00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.151
and a millisecond and a quarter late,

145
00:04:56.151 --> 00:04:58.325
against their usual delay, comfortably

146
00:04:58.325 --> 00:05:00.181
inside the five-millisecond margin.

147
00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.937
Besides the audio's own share, two things

148
00:05:05.937 --> 00:05:06.710
are unmeasured.

149
00:05:07.019 --> 00:05:09.131
First, how long the board takes from a

150
00:05:09.131 --> 00:05:10.522
pluck to sending its onset.

151
00:05:10.831 --> 00:05:13.098
There are three figures in our documents,

152
00:05:13.098 --> 00:05:14.901
and none was measured on the board.

153
00:05:15.617 --> 00:05:17.799
That number matters, because the same

154
00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:19.928
twenty milliseconds is taken off every

155
00:05:19.928 --> 00:05:21.183
pluck's estimated time.

156
00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.693
If the real delay is different, every

157
00:05:23.693 --> 00:05:25.930
estimate is off by the difference, and

158
00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.113
plucks near a rounding edge land a slot

159
00:05:28.113 --> 00:05:29.259
early or a slot late.

160
00:05:29.719 --> 00:05:32.064
Second, the whole thing: from a pluck to

161
00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:32.944
the drum you hear.

162
00:05:33.237 --> 00:05:35.289
That's never been measured, because the

163
00:05:35.289 --> 00:05:37.243
real guitar hasn't yet driven the drums

164
00:05:37.243 --> 00:05:37.487
live.

165
00:05:37.781 --> 00:05:39.686
The tools to measure the board's delay

166
00:05:39.686 --> 00:05:41.933
already exist: record single plucks with

167
00:05:41.933 --> 00:05:44.083
the microphone listening, and run the mic

168
00:05:44.083 --> 00:05:44.376
check.

169
00:05:46.013 --> 00:05:48.204
So at a hundred and twenty the laptop has

170
00:05:48.204 --> 00:05:50.498
thirty-seven and a half milliseconds, the

171
00:05:50.498 --> 00:05:52.845
audio needs thirty, and the median step is

172
00:05:52.845 --> 00:05:53.992
no guide to the worst.

173
00:05:54.305 --> 00:05:56.444
For every number in the report: say

174
00:05:56.444 --> 00:05:58.842
whether it was measured, reported, or not

175
00:05:58.842 --> 00:05:59.520
yet measured.

